{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act criminal-conviction eviction grounds","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of assault or terrorist threats against the landlord, family or employees, theft of property or human trafficking, or found civilly liable for theft, assault or drug activity on the premises; a three-day Notice to Quit is required.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; 2-year limit on filing","coverage_conditions":"Tenants convicted of, or who pleaded guilty to, the listed offenses, juveniles adjudicated delinquent for such offenses, and tenants who knowingly permit such a person to reside at the premises.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental units covered by the Anti-Eviction Act"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"Also, if the tenant permits a person he knows has been convicted of or has pleaded guilty to these actions to reside at the premises continuously or occasionally, the landlord may file for eviction. A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least three days prior to filing suit for eviction (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1(q)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings may be evicted only by court order and only for the listed causes, including holding over, nonpayment of rent, disorderly conduct, willful damage and rule violations. Notice to quit: three months (at-will or year-to-year), one month (month-to-month).","key_value":"3 months' notice to quit (at-will/year-to-year); 1 month (month-to-month)","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings; removal requires a court order and proof of a listed cause.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction good cause and notice","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rooming and boarding houses; units without private kitchens and bathrooms"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled (N.J.S.A. 55:13B-3).","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"There is a further requirement that the owner give at least three (3) working days’ notice to the County Welfare Board before starting the eviction action for any resident (N.J.A.C. 5:26-3.4(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-reprisal protection for tenants' civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by eviction, substantial lease alteration or refusal to renew when a tenant exercises lease or legal rights, complains in good faith to a government authority about health or safety violations, or joins a tenant organization. Tenant may sue for damages.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"all rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations","requirement":"A landlord who evicts to personally occupy a unit must occupy it at least six months, and a landlord who evicts to permanently board up, demolish or retire a building from residential use may not use the property residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar","coverage_conditions":"Units vacated after notice that the landlord will personally occupy the unit, or after notice that the building is being permanently boarded up, demolished or retired from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; for removal from residential use also a civil penalty of up to $10,000 per violation.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction protections and 90-day notice","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction. A buyer who purchases a foreclosed property for personal occupancy must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit, and must notify tenants in English and Spanish within 10 business days after the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days to notify tenants","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental units in foreclosure; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental units in foreclosure"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice requirements.","interaction":"Federal foreclosure law does not preempt State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Tenants at least 62 years old or permanently disabled who lived in the building a year before the conversion recording date may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is not more than three times county average per-person income or $50,000.","key_value":"40-year protected tenancy; income limit 3x county average or $50,000","coverage_conditions":"Buildings converted or being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership; tenants must meet age, disability and one-year residency tests.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy","requirement":"Extends protected tenancy to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties — at present the only qualified county is Hudson County.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Hudson County conversion buildings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rent increase above rent control limit or unconscionable increase as prohibited conduct","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent above any rent control or rent leveling ordinance limit, and where the property is not rent-controlled an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase is listed as prohibited conduct.","key_value":"increase above rent control limit; unreasonable or unconscionable increase where uncontrolled","coverage_conditions":"All residential rental property; the provision refers to rent control or rent leveling ordinances where they apply and to uncontrolled property.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"all residential rentals; rent control or rent leveling ordinances where enacted"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Cmty. Affairs, Truth in Renting (provision not identified in this part)","quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.4,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
